    "blueprint": "THE QUOTATION\n\n  \"two hard things: cache invalidation and naming\n   things\" -- commonly attributed to Phil Karlton,\n  NOT firmly documented to a source. the numbered\n  variants are LATER ADDITIONS.\n\nTHE SYSTEMS\n\n  2003  Memcached, for LiveJournal: web servers POOL\n        SPARE MEMORY into a shared layer.\n  2009  Redis: a DATA STRUCTURE store -- lists, sets,\n        sorted sets, optional persistence.\n        -> once it holds STRUCTURES it stops being\n           only a cache and becomes SOMEWHERE STATE\n           LIVES.\n\nTHE WRITE SIDE IS THE WHOLE DIFFICULTY\n\n  two representations of ONE FACT.\n  db first    -> stale until cleared\n  cache first -> a concurrent read RESTORES the stale\n                 value, WITH A FRESH EXPIRY\n  both        -> the second can fail alone. no error\n                 describes it.\n  a timer     -> you chose how long users see the old\n                 answer\n\n  NONE IS A BUG. all are the SAME inconsistency,\n  DISTRIBUTED DIFFERENTLY.\n\nTHE STAMPEDE\n\n  a hot item expires -> everyone misses AT ONCE ->\n  all regenerate THE SAME THING\n  \"cascading failure\": concurrent recomputations\n  LENGTHEN each recomputation, PULLING MORE REQUESTS\n  IN.\n  -> THE LOAD IS GENERATED BY THE RECOVERY.\n  -> and the key most worth caching is the one whose\n     expiry is WORST.\n\nTHE REMEDY, AND THE RANKING\n\n  BEST     refresh randomly BEFORE expiry, chance\n           rising as it nears. shown OPTIMAL. NO\n           LOCKS. no gap ever opens.\n  lock     removes duplicate work; everyone still\n           waits; a lock in the hot path\n  bg warm  works; needs a second system that knows\n           which keys matter\n  stale    best product answer WHEN old data is\n           acceptable -- which is the original\n           question.",
    "takeaway": "A cache creates two representations of one fact, so every write ordering distributes the same unavoidable staleness differently rather than removing it — and the stampede is worst for the key most worth caching, which is why the published fix refreshes randomly before expiry so no gap ever opens."
