    "blueprint": "THE THREE INDISTINGUISHABLE CASES\n\n   no response means one of:\n      never arrived\n      arrived and failed\n      ARRIVED AND SUCCEEDED, reply lost\n\n   -> retry is safe only if twice == once.\n\nTHE DEFINITION\n\n   idempotent = \"the INTENDED EFFECT on the server\n   of multiple identical requests ... is the same\n   as the effect for a single such request\"\n\n   replacement, deletion, safe methods.\n\n   about STATE. not the response, not the logs.\n\n   \"though the response might differ\" --\n   deleting an already-deleted thing may reply\n   differently. still idempotent.\n\nTHE PROHIBITION\n\n   SHOULD NOT auto-retry a non-idempotent method\n   unless you KNOW the semantics are idempotent,\n   or can DETECT the original never applied.\n\n   \"some clients take a riskier approach and\n    attempt to guess\"\n   -> that is what a double charge is.\n\nMAKING THE UNREPEATABLE REPEATABLE\n\n   caller supplies an ATTEMPT KEY\n   server records APPLIED keys\n   a repeat returns the ORIGINAL OUTCOME\n\nTHE CAVEAT NOBODY MENTIONS\n\n   this is NOT exactly-once.\n   keys are remembered for a FINITE time.\n   a late retry looks like a NEW request.\n\n   AT-MOST-ONCE WITHIN A WINDOW.\n   the window is a decision.",
    "takeaway": "A lost response is indistinguishable from work that never happened, so retrying is safe only when the intended effect of many requests equals that of one — and attempt keys buy you at-most-once within a retention window rather than exactly-once."
