        "answer": "They are the two identities that move a negation across a bracket, changing the operator as it goes. The negation of a disjunction equals the conjunction of the negations — not one-or-the-other is neither. The negation of a conjunction equals the disjunction of the negations — not both is at least one missing.\n\nSo the negation of 'is an administrator or is an editor' becomes 'is not an administrator and is not an editor'.\n\nWhy it matters is not elegance. A negated compound makes a reader hold the inner expression in mind, evaluate it, and then invert the result — three steps, with the inversion applied to something just constructed mentally. Pushed inwards, each term is a fact checkable on its own and the combination is one step. That is the difference between a condition people get right and one they argue about.\n\nIf the identity is not obvious it should be verified rather than trusted: two variables give four combinations, so writing both expressions across all four settles it in under a minute — faster than the discussion it replaces, and not dependent on anyone's recollection. The same technique handles the cases that cause real bugs; three variables give eight rows, which is still tractable by hand and settles precedence and boundary arguments definitively.\n\nI would state the limitation, because the algebra is not quite the language. It assumes both operands are simply true or false, while real conditions short-circuit — if the first operand of a conjunction is false, the second is never evaluated. So an expression the algebra calls equivalent may not be equivalent in code: reordering can change whether a side effect happens, whether a null is dereferenced, or whether an expensive call is made. Applying these laws changes the operator, which changes which operand guards which, and that is precisely where a mechanical rewrite introduces a fault the truth table cannot show.\n\nWhat makes any of this trustworthy is the identification underneath: two-valued algebra and switching circuits are the same structure, so manipulating the expression really is manipulating the circuit."
