    "blueprint": "1854  logic becomes ALGEBRA -- variables with TWO\n      VALUES, laws that can be PROVED.\n1937  a master's thesis connects it to MACHINERY,\n      and the correspondence is EXACT:\n        SERIES   -> AND   (both closed)\n        PARALLEL -> OR    (either closed)\n\nWHAT THAT BUYS\n\n  before  arrange switches until it LOOKS right;\n          check by TESTING\n  after   A CIRCUIT IS AN EXPRESSION\n          -> prove equivalence WITHOUT BUILDING\n          -> simplify by algebra = FEWER PARTS, same\n             behaviour\n  -> made it possible to design circuits TOO COMPLEX\n     TO ARRANGE.\n\nTHE OPERATIONS\n\n  AND  both     OR  AT LEAST one     NOT  flips\n  XOR  they differ <- what everyday \"or\" usually\n                      means\n\nTHE TWO LAWS\n\n  NOT(a OR b)  = NOT a AND NOT b     (\"neither\")\n  NOT(a AND b) = NOT a OR  NOT b     (\"one missing\")\n\n  a negated compound = hold it, evaluate it, INVERT\n  IT.\n  pushed inwards = each term checkable ALONE.\n\nTHE HABIT THAT BEATS THE IDENTITIES\n\n  ENUMERATE. 2 vars = 4 rows. 3 = 8.\n  settles precedence and edge cases FASTER THAN THE\n  ARGUMENT,\n  and does not depend on THE REASONER BEING RIGHT.\n\nTHE LIMITATION\n\n  the algebra assumes plain true/false.\n  CODE SHORT-CIRCUITS.\n  -> \"equivalent\" expressions differ in whether a\n     side effect runs, a null is dereferenced, a\n     call is made.\n  -> changing the operator CHANGES WHICH OPERAND\n     GUARDS WHICH.",
    "takeaway": "Series is conjunction and parallel is disjunction, exactly — which turned circuits into expressions that can be proved and reduced without being built; push negations inward so each term is checkable alone, enumerate when the variables are few, and remember the algebra ignores short-circuiting, so an equivalent expression can still behave differently."
