    "title": "Contrapositive: why debugging is contrapositive reasoning",
    "story": "A conditional says that if the first thing holds, the second follows. Three rearrangements of it are available and only one of them is safe.\n\nSwapping the two halves gives the converse, which does not follow. Negating both halves in place gives the inverse, which does not follow either. Negating both halves and swapping them gives the contrapositive, and that one is not merely plausible — it is the same statement. If the second thing is absent, the first cannot have held. Anything true of the original is true of it, because they are one claim written two ways.\n\nThat is the entire logical content of debugging, and it is worth seeing why rather than being told.\n\nConsider an invariant of the kind every system has: if this function completed correctly, then the stored balance was updated. During an incident you cannot watch the function run. What you can do is look at the stored balance, and the balance was not updated. The contrapositive converts that observation into a conclusion with no inference gap at all: the function did not complete correctly. Not probably. Not likely. The observation and the conclusion are the same fact.\n\nNotice what makes this powerful, because it is not the certainty on its own. It is that the observation is available and the thing you wanted to know is not. You cannot inspect execution after the fact; you can inspect state. The contrapositive is the move that converts the evidence you have into a claim about the thing you cannot see, which is the position every post-hoc investigation starts from.\n\nAnd the failure mode is the mirror image. Seeing the effect and concluding the suspected cause is the converse, which does not follow — the balance being wrong is consistent with that function failing and with several other things. An investigation built on the converse feels identical from the inside: a hypothesis, a symptom that matches, hours spent in a subsystem that was working the whole time. The symptom matching is not evidence, because a wrong hypothesis predicting the symptom is exactly as compatible with what you observed.\n\nSo the discipline is to phrase each step as an elimination rather than a nomination. Not 'this looks like the cause' but 'if this were working, that would be present; it is absent; so this is not working'. Each such step removes a region of the search space permanently, which is progress you keep. Nominating a suspect and looking for confirmation produces evidence that is equally consistent with being wrong, which is why it can go on for hours without converging.\n\nOne limit, because certainty at the logical level is easy to overtrust. The conclusion is only as good as the invariant. If the premise was never actually true of the system — the function can complete correctly and fail to update the balance, because another writer overwrote it, or the update is deferred, or the read was from a replica — then the deduction is valid and its conclusion is still wrong. The reasoning does not fail; the premise does. So the first question about a confident elimination is not whether the logic holds but whether the conditional it rests on was ever true.",
