      "move": "State the invariant as a conditional, then use the absence of its consequence to eliminate its premise. Phrase every step as 'if this were working, that would be true; it is not; so this is not working', and check the conditional itself before trusting any conclusion drawn from it.",
      "invariant": "Negating both halves of a conditional and swapping them produces the same claim, so the absence of the consequence is proof of the absence of the premise. Swapping without negating, or negating without swapping, proves nothing.",
      "breaks": "A valid deduction from a false premise gives a confident wrong answer. If the invariant was never true of the system — another writer overwrites the value, the update is deferred, the read came from a replica — the logic holds and the conclusion is still false, and the certainty makes it harder to revisit than a guess would have been.",
