    "blueprint": "FOUR ARRANGEMENTS, ONE SAFE\n\n  original        first -> second\n  converse        swap                 NO\n  inverse         negate in place      NO\n  CONTRAPOSITIVE  negate AND swap      SAME CLAIM\n\nDEBUGGING IS THE THIRD ONE\n\n  invariant   completed correctly -> balance updated\n  observed    balance NOT updated\n  therefore   DID NOT complete correctly\n\n  no inference gap. the observation and the\n  conclusion are ONE FACT, WRITTEN TWICE.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: THE DIRECTION\n\n  evidence about STATE. question about EXECUTION.\n  this is the move that crosses between them.\n\nTHE MIRROR-IMAGE MISTAKE\n\n  symptom matches hypothesis -> \"found it\"\n  = swap without negate. PROVES NOTHING.\n  a WRONG hypothesis predicting the symptom is\n  EQUALLY compatible.\n\nELIMINATE, DO NOT NOMINATE\n\n  each elimination removes search space PERMANENTLY\n  -> converges\n  nomination rules nothing out\n  -> can run forever\n\nTHE LIMIT\n\n  valid deduction + false invariant = CONFIDENT\n  WRONG ANSWER.\n  ask whether the conditional was EVER TRUE.",
    "takeaway": "Only one rearrangement of a conditional preserves it: if the consequence is absent, the premise did not hold — which is what lets you argue from state you can see to execution you cannot, and why every step should eliminate rather than nominate, as long as you check the invariant was ever true."
