    "beats": {
      "broke": "A model was promoted as an artificial brain on the strength of what it could learn, with nobody having characterised what it could not represent at any weights.",
      "fix": "Establish the limit formally — one threshold unit divides its input space with a single hyperplane — then add a hidden layer with a non-linear activation so earlier layers reshape the space until a linear boundary suffices.",
      "cost": "The result was about one layer and was widely received as being about the whole approach, and the popular history of what followed is a stronger causal claim than the evidence supports.",
      "interview": {
        "q": "Why can a single-layer perceptron not compute exclusive-or, and what removes the limitation?",
        "trap": "Describing it as a training problem. No learning rate, quantity of data or patience helps, because no weights exist that compute the function — it is outside the model's range rather than hard to find within it.",
        "answer": "Because one threshold unit divides its input space with a single hyperplane, and the two classes of exclusive-or cannot be put on opposite sides of any line.\n\nWork it: four input combinations, the matching pairs giving one answer and the mismatched pairs the other. Plotted on a square, the classes occupy opposite diagonals. A line splitting the square would have to put both matching corners on one side and both mismatched on the other, and those pairs are the diagonals — no line separates a diagonal.\n\nThe general distinction is representable versus learnable. This is not a training failure; there is no learning rate or quantity of data that helps, because no weights and bias exist that compute it. That is a different kind of failure from a model that could express the answer and has not found it.\n\nThe remedy is a hidden layer with a non-linear activation: the earlier layer reshapes the space so a linear boundary in the final layer suffices. The non-linearity is essential, because composing linear maps gives a linear map — a hundred stacked linear layers compute exactly what one computes, so without it depth buys nothing.\n\nOn the history I would be careful, because the usual telling contains a stronger causal claim than the evidence. The received story is that the 1969 book proved neural networks were a dead end and the field went dark for fifteen years. That is contested — Olazaran's 1996 study is the canonical treatment. The authors had been arguing against neural networks since around 1965, so the book was a culmination rather than an opening shot; its content concerns single-layer perceptrons; many researchers had already left by 1969; Rosenblatt died in 1971. The defensible version is weaker: the critique, with its authors' standing, significantly influenced a temporary stall in research in the United States. And the dates do not fit — the first funding collapse is mid-1970s, following the 1973 Lighthill report and the Department of Defense study.\n\nWhat I would take from it is the well-evidenced part, which is more interesting than the myth: a precise result about single-layer perceptrons was received as a result about the whole approach, and that gap did real damage."
      }
    },
    "blueprint": "1958  a weighted sum + bias through a THRESHOLD\n      -> ONE HYPERPLANE dividing the input space\n      the Navy's announcement: it will \"walk, talk,\n      see, write, reproduce itself and be conscious\n      of its existence\"\n\n1969  THE LIMIT, WORKED\n\n      exclusive-or: matching pairs -> one answer,\n      mismatched -> the other\n      on a square, THE CLASSES ARE OPPOSITE DIAGONALS\n      a line must split matching from mismatched\n      NO LINE SEPARATES A DIAGONAL\n\n      -> no weights exist. REPRESENTABLE, not\n         LEARNABLE.\n         no learning rate or data helps.\n\nTHE HISTORY -- AND HERE THE USUAL TELLING IS WRONG\n\n  received: the book killed the field for 15 years\n  contested: canonical treatment is a 1996 study in\n  Social Studies of Science\n\n    arguing against neural nets SINCE ~1965 -> a\n    CULMINATION, not an opening shot\n    content is about SINGLE-LAYER perceptrons\n    most researchers had ALREADY LEFT by 1969\n    Rosenblatt DIED IN 1971\n    the first funding collapse is MID-1970s, after\n    the 1973 LIGHTHILL report and the DoD study\n\n  defensible version: the critique, with its authors'\n  standing, significantly influenced a TEMPORARY\n  STALL in US research.\n\n  the well-evidenced and more interesting fact:\n  A PRECISE RESULT ABOUT ONE LAYER WAS RECEIVED AS A\n  RESULT ABOUT THE WHOLE APPROACH.\n\nTHE FIX\n\n  hidden layer + NON-LINEAR activation\n  -> reshape the space until a line suffices\n  and the non-linearity is not decoration:\n  LINEAR o LINEAR = LINEAR.\n  a hundred linear layers = ONE.",
    "takeaway": "One threshold unit draws one hyperplane, so exclusive-or is outside its range at any weights — a correct result about a single layer that was received as a result about the whole approach, and the popular claim that it caused the field's winter is contested, with the funding collapse following 1973 reports rather than the 1969 book."
