      "claim": "The Unicode project began in 1987 when Joe Becker, an engineer at Xerox, drafted a proposal for a universal 16-bit character set, and his report Unicode 88 was issued on 29 August 1988. It described Unicode as addressing the need for a reliable world text encoding, roughly a wide-body ASCII stretched to 16 bits to encompass the characters of the world's living languages, and argued that 16 bits per character were more than sufficient — that 65,536 code points would be enough for every character needed for modern communication. The work grew out of eight years of experience with Xerox's internal Character Code Standard, XCCS.",
